  "7517949203004743682": {
   "created": 1792418766,
   "author": "Thomas Schutzius",
   "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/activity-7517949203004743682-DK3Z",
   "text": "We had an incredible session in our Next-Generation Data Centers and Energy Management Systems course at UC Berkeley, featuring a guest lecture from Travis Gaskill, Lead Thermal Engineer at Etched. Drawing on his experience at both Etched and NVIDIA (where he helped pioneer the liquid-cooled NVL72 architecture), Travis walked us through \"A Hardware-Centric Look at AI and the Thermal Bottlenecks on the Horizon.\"\n\nHere are a few standout takeaways from his deep dive into the physical limits of AI compute:\n\n- Density drives liquid cooling: We didn't move to liquid cooling just to remove heat. We moved to liquid cooling because interconnecting chips need to be physically as close as possible to ",
